<header><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">Module 1 • Pre-Lecture Reading</div><h1>Inside the Inside</h1>
<p class="lead">A game has 3 worlds, and each world has 4 checkpoints. At every checkpoint, the game may make another decision: is the player carrying a key, and if so, is it the right color? Programs often need to repeat <em>inside</em> repetition and decide <em>inside</em> decisions.</p>
<div class="meta"><span class="pill">~16–18 minutes</span><span class="pill">Interactive</span><span class="pill">No Python setup needed</span><span class="pill">Saves locally</span></div></div></header>
<div class="sticky"><div class="track"><div id="bar" class="bar"></div></div><p>Goal: trace how nested loops repeat and how nested conditionals decide which inner branch can run.</p></div>
<main>
<section><h2>1. Vocabulary: nesting language</h2><p>Flip each card before tracing code.</p><div class="grid">
<button type="button" class="card term"><span class="front"><strong>nested</strong><br><span class="small">Flip</span></span><span class="back"><strong>Nested</strong><br>Placed inside another structure.</span></button>
<button type="button" class="card term"><span class="front"><strong>outer loop</strong><br><span class="small">Flip</span></span><span class="back"><strong>Outer loop</strong><br>The surrounding loop. Each outer pass starts a complete run of its inner loop.</span></button>
<button type="button" class="card term"><span class="front"><strong>inner loop</strong><br><span class="small">Flip</span></span><span class="back"><strong>Inner loop</strong><br>The loop inside another loop. It completes all of its passes for each outer pass.</span></button>
<button type="button" class="card term"><span class="front"><strong>branch</strong><br><span class="small">Flip</span></span><span class="back"><strong>Branch</strong><br>A path selected by a conditional. An inner conditional is only reached if execution enters its outer branch.</span></button>
</div></section>

<section><h2>2. Recall before nesting</h2><div class="card"><p><strong>How many values does <code>range(5)</code> produce?</strong></p>
<button type="button" class="choice recall" data-a="4">4</button><button type="button" class="choice recall" data-a="5">5</button><button type="button" class="choice recall" data-a="6">6</button><div id="recfb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div>
<p class="callout">A nested loop does not change how <code>range()</code> works. First find each loop's count correctly; then reason about how those counts combine.</p></section>

<section><h2>3. One complete inner run per outer pass</h2><p>Think of a playlist app: for each playlist category, inspect every mood tag before moving to the next category.</p>
<div class="trace"><div class="step">Outer A<br>Inner 1</div><div class="step">Outer A<br>Inner 2</div><div class="step">Outer B<br>Inner 1</div><div class="step">Outer B<br>Inner 2</div></div>
<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem"><p><strong>Which description matches nested-loop execution?</strong></p>
<button type="button" class="choice orderq" data-a="complete">The inner loop finishes all its passes before the outer loop advances.</button>
<button type="button" class="choice orderq" data-a="alternate">The outer and inner loops take turns advancing one pass each.</button>
<button type="button" class="choice orderq" data-a="once">The inner loop runs only once total.</button><div id="orderfb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div></section>

<section><h2>4. Predict the total</h2><div class="code"><div id="loopScreen" class="screen">for aisle in range(4):
    for bin_num in range(2):
        print(aisle, bin_num)</div><div class="codeactions"><button type="button" id="looprun">Reveal simulated output</button></div></div>
<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem"><p><strong>Before revealing: how many lines should print?</strong></p>
<button type="button" class="choice countq" data-a="8">8</button><button type="button" class="choice countq" data-a="6">6</button><button type="button" class="choice countq" data-a="4">4</button><div id="countfb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div></section>

<section><h2>5. Indentation shows what is inside</h2><div class="code"><div class="screen">for team in range(2):
    for round_num in range(3):
        print("play")
    print("team done")</div></div>
<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem"><p><strong>How many times does <code>"team done"</code> print?</strong></p>
<button type="button" class="choice indentq" data-a="2">2</button><button type="button" class="choice indentq" data-a="3">3</button><button type="button" class="choice indentq" data-a="6">6</button><div id="indentfb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div>
<p class="warn"><strong>Trace indentation before arithmetic.</strong> The second print is inside the outer loop but outside the inner loop, so it runs once per outer pass.</p></section>

<section><h2>6. Nested conditionals are gated decisions</h2><div class="tree"><strong>Outer question:</strong> Is the account active?<div class="inner"><strong>Only if yes:</strong> Does it have premium access?</div></div>
<p>The inner question is not automatically checked. Python must first enter the outer branch that contains it.</p>
<div class="card"><p><strong>If the account is inactive, what happens to the premium check?</strong></p>
<button type="button" class="choice gateq" data-a="skip">It is skipped because execution never enters that outer branch.</button><button type="button" class="choice gateq" data-a="check">It still runs because every <code>if</code> must be tested.</button><button type="button" class="choice gateq" data-a="error">Python raises an error because the inner check was not used.</button><div id="gatefb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div></section>

<section><h2>7. Trace the branch that actually runs</h2><div class="code"><div id="condScreen" class="screen">status = "ACTIVE"
points = 7

if status == "ACTIVE":
    if points >= 10:
        print("bonus")
    else:
        print("standard")
else:
    print("inactive")</div><div class="codeactions"><button type="button" id="condrun">Reveal simulated output</button></div></div>
<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem"><p><strong>What should print?</strong></p>
<button type="button" class="choice condq" data-a="standard"><code>standard</code></button><button type="button" class="choice condq" data-a="bonus"><code>bonus</code></button><button type="button" class="choice condq" data-a="inactive"><code>inactive</code></button><div id="condfb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div></section>

<section><h2>8. Combine repetition + decisions</h2><p>A loop can process each item while a nested conditional decides what should happen to that item. You do not need the project's data to practice the shape.</p>
<div class="code"><div class="screen">alerts = [("EMAIL", True), ("SMS", False)]

for channel, enabled in alerts:
    if channel == "EMAIL":
        if enabled:
            print("send email")
        else:
            print("email paused")</div></div>
<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem"><p><strong>For the <code>("SMS", False)</code> item, is <code>if enabled</code> evaluated?</strong></p>
<button type="button" class="choice comboq" data-a="no">No. The outer <code>channel == "EMAIL"</code> condition is false.</button><button type="button" class="choice comboq" data-a="yes">Yes. The loop guarantees every inner conditional runs.</button><div id="combofb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div></section>

<section><h2>9. Debugging habit: trace outside → inside</h2><div class="card"><p>An inner message never appears. What should you inspect <em>first</em>?</p>
<button type="button" class="choice debugq" data-a="outer">Whether execution ever enters the outer branch containing the inner check.</button><button type="button" class="choice debugq" data-a="random">Whether Python randomly skipped the inner condition.</button><button type="button" class="choice debugq" data-a="import">Whether nested conditionals need an import.</button><div id="debugfb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div>
<label for="strategy"><strong>Your trace strategy:</strong> In 1–2 sentences, explain how you would trace unfamiliar nested code without guessing.</label><textarea id="strategy" data-save placeholder="Try starting with: “First I identify the outer structure…”"></textarea><p><button type="button" id="hintbtn">Generate a thinking hint</button></p><div id="hint" class="hint"></div></section>

<section class="bridge"><h2>10. Required bridge to lecture + project</h2><p>You just practiced the two reasoning habits the upcoming lesson targets: count a nested loop by following complete inner runs, and trace a nested conditional from the outside inward to determine which branch is reachable. In lecture/lab, you will use those habits while stepping through live Python. Later, the project applies the same overall shape in <code>validate_line</code>: repeated records plus decisions inside that repeated work.</p>
<div class="card"><p><strong>Bridge challenge — new situation:</strong> A program checks every support ticket. Only tickets labeled <code>"URGENT"</code> receive a second check for whether the customer is currently waiting. What should you trace first for one ticket?</p>
<button type="button" class="choice bridgeq" data-a="label">First determine whether the ticket enters the <code>"URGENT"</code> outer branch.</button><button type="button" class="choice bridgeq" data-a="waiting">Always check whether the customer is waiting first.</button><button type="button" class="choice bridgeq" data-a="all">Assume both conditions run for every ticket.</button><div id="bridgefb" class="feedback" tabindex="-1"></div></div>
<label for="bring"><strong>Bring to lecture:</strong> What visual clue in Python code tells you whether a statement belongs to the outer structure, the inner structure, or neither?</label><textarea id="bring" data-save></textarea></section>

<section><h2>11. Keep a copy of your work</h2><div class="export"><div class="actions"><button type="button" id="md">Create Markdown file</button><button type="button" id="json">Create JSON file</button><button type="button" id="txt">Create TXT file</button><button type="button" id="show">Show plain text</button></div><p id="exportfb" class="small" aria-live="polite"></p><div id="plainwrap" class="plain"><label for="plain"><strong>Plain-text fallback</strong></label><textarea id="plain" style="min-height:260px"></textarea><p><button type="button" id="selectall">Select all text</button></p></div></div></section>
</main>
<script>
const $=s=>document.querySelector(s),$$=s=>[...document.querySelectorAll(s)];let history=[];const KEY="nested-loops-conditionals-reading-v1";
function save(){localStorage.setItem(KEY,JSON.stringify({responses:Object.fromEntries($$("[data-save]").map(x=>[x.id,x.value])),history}))}
